Omega SREP 3.0.0 (November 10, 2026)
huge-dictionary LZ77 preprocessor (Omega lineage, fork of SREP by Bulat Ziganshin)
https://github.com/YadeWira/omega-srep

Usage:
  osrep [options] -mN  input  output       compress
  osrep [options] -d   archive output      decompress
  osrep -i archive                         show archive info
  osrep --verify archive                   check it without unpacking

Compression methods:
  -m0       REP only (in-memory; works with -dBYTES)
  -m1, -m2  Content-defined chunking (CDC)
  -m3       Digest-comparison match search (default)
  -m4       Full match search (best ratio for general data)
  -m5       Exhaustive match search (slowest, best ratio)

Common options:
  -lN, -cN          minimum-match / chunk-size tuning
  -aN[/M]           match-finder accelerator (-m3/-m4/-m5): N bytes
                    of bit array per chunk, and the scan variant M
                    (default N, at most 16); -a0 turns it off.
                    Like -l/-c, it changes the archive.
  -ia-, -ia+        -ia- turns off the slice filter of -m5 (and of
                    -m4 with -c well below -l); -ia+, the default,
                    keeps it. Like -l/-c, it changes the archive.
  -bN               buffer size (default 8mb)
  -dBYTES           dictionary size for -m0
  -hash=NAME        select hash (vmac, sha1, ...)
  -mmap, -nommap    enable/disable POSIX mmap reads
  -temp=PATH        tempfile path override
  --format=v4|v6    container to write (default v6). v6 is the current
                    format; v4 is the container the 1.0.x releases
                    read, kept for interoperability. v1-v5 are always
                    readable; v5 is not written anymore.
  --seed=N          deterministic hash seed (uint64; default is
                    random per-run). Same seed + same input ->
                    byte-identical archive. Useful for content-
                    addressed storage and reproducible builds.
  -bar              emit "PROGRESS <done> <total>" to stderr every
                    ~0.5s, plus a guaranteed final line at
                    done==total. Machine-parseable, for wrapping
                    osrep as a subprocess.

Dedup pre-pass (Omega F5):
  -dup              enable FA-style dedup pre-pass
  --dup-paranoid    byte-compare on every dedup hit (slower, no
                    silent corruption on 64-bit hash collision)
  --chunk-avg=N     CDC average chunk size (default 4096)
  --chunk-min=N     CDC minimum chunk size (default 1024)
  --chunk-max=N     CDC maximum chunk size (default 16384)
  --chunk-buf=N     buffer-bounded CDC, bytes (default 8388608)
  --chunk-hash=NAME CDC boundary hash: fnv (default) or gear.
                    gear = Gear-hash CDC with an implicit ~64-byte
                    content window (FastCDC-style normalized
                    chunking); finds non-buffer-aligned duplicates
                    that fnv's unwindowed rolling hash misses.
                    Opt-in; does not change the .dupref format.

Accepted and not acted on:
  -tN               the implementation is single-threaded per block,
                    so there is no worker count to set
  -mmap, -nommap    mmap reads
  -slp, -slp-       large-page mode
  -pc[N]            progress counters
  These are parsed and validated so scripts written against the
  1.0.x C++ keep working unchanged. None of them changes the
  archive: that was measured before the port, not assumed.

Checking:
  --verify          v5 only: check an archive's framing, CRCs and
                    records without decompressing it. Catches
                    truncation and framing damage in the time it
                    takes to read the file. It does NOT cover the
                    stored block bytes -- damage inside a literal
                    run still needs a -d. v1-v4 carry no checksum
                    at all and are refused.

Help:
  --help, -h, -?    this synopsis
  --version, -V     version line

Project page: https://github.com/YadeWira/omega-srep
